  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building skills and resilience of vulnerable women affected by COVID-19 in South Lebanon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon&apos;s political crisis and economic downturn, exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic, has impacted heavily on living standards both for Lebanese and for the large numbers of refugees and displaced persons currently in Lebanon. 
A high percentage of the population of South Lebanon is employed in the informal sector with no social security or legal protection and usually low-paid, resulting in widespread poverty.  Housing conditions are often poor and overcrowded, and often shared with other families.  Relations between host community and the Syrian or Iraqi refugees, and between different religious groups, is complex, and the deteriorating economic situation has resulted in rising tensions. Within communities, domestic violence is known to have increased during the pandemic.  The project will work in three parishes to set up committees each comprising six prospective women community leaders who will be given training and then mobilised to provide information and awareness sessions to vulnerable women on how to cope with stress and care for their mental health.  After the awareness sessions participants will receive hygiene kits.</narrative>
  </description>
